00:09:10.460 --> 00:09:16.680
get wrong and it cost me money before I figured it out. Imagine you bought a Canadian bank ETF

00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:22.900
last month expecting steady income only to realize the price already baked in the next quarter's hike.

00:09:23.460 --> 00:09:30.320
The key distinction is between current yield and dividend growth rate. A 4% yield on a stock with

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:37.300
flat payouts delivers less long-term compounding than a 2% yield growing 8% annually because

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:44.200
reinvested dividends accelerate position size over 5 to 10 years. The dividend tax credit in

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:49.940
a non-registered account can reduce the effective tax rate on eligible Canadian dividends to roughly

00:09:49.940 --> 00:09:58.440
15 to 20% for middle income investors versus 30% or more on interest income. The pro tip most

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:03.600
people miss is checking the payout ratio against free cash flow rather than just earnings.

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:11.280
Sustainable policies usually sit below 70% of FCF giving a buffer during downturns.

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:17.060
Reads add another layer as income vehicles but carry interest rate sensitivity that requires

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:23.320
separate allocation limits in a TFSA. The biggest mistake with dividend strategies

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:29.080
is chasing yield traps that cut payouts during recessions. Always filter first for

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:34.780
consistent growth histories and FCF coverage before comparing yields across account types.

